1. TCP Server using fork() (if applicable):
Server:


gcc fork_server.c -o fork_server
./fork_server
Client:


gcc fork_client.c -o fork_client
./fork_client
This implementation uses fork() to handle each new client in a separate process.

Useful for basic concurrent models; incurs overhead for each process created.

2. TCP Server using select()
Server:


gcc select_server.c -o select_server
./select_server
Client:


gcc client.c -o client
./client
The server handles multiple clients using select() for I/O multiplexing.

Single-threaded and efficient for handling many connections without forking.

3. Multithreaded TCP Server
Server:


gcc multithreaded_server.c -o multithreaded_server -lpthread
./multithreaded_server
Client:


# Change PORT to 8081 in client.c
gcc client.c -o client
./client
Creates a new thread (pthread) for each client.

Enables true parallelism, but requires careful memory and thread management.

4. Blocking I/O

gcc blocking_io.c -o blocking_io
./blocking_io
Waits (blocks) for user input before proceeding.

Simple but not suitable for high-performance servers.

5. Non-blocking I/O

gcc nonblocking_io.c -o nonblocking_io
./nonblocking_io
Allows the program to continue even when no input is available.

Ideal for event-driven or real-time applications.

6. Multiplexing I/O

gcc multiplexing_io.c -o multiplexing_io
./multiplexing_io
Client:


# Change PORT to 8082 in client.c
gcc client.c -o client
./client
Combines keyboard input and client socket handling using select().

Efficient for managing multiple input streams in one process.

7. Signal-Driven I/O

gcc signal_driven_io.c -o signal_driven_io
./signal_driven_io
Uses system signals like SIGIO to handle input events.

A more asynchronous way of handling input readiness.

8. Signal Handling in Network Program

gcc signal_handler.c -o signal_handler
./signal_handler
Handles interrupt signals like SIGINT (Ctrl+C) gracefully.

Ensures resources are released and the program exits cleanly.

9. Daemonizing a Process

gcc daemon_process.c -o daemon_process
./daemon_process &
Runs in the background as a system daemon.

Logs activity every 10 seconds to /tmp/daemon_log.txt.

To view logs:


tail -f /tmp/daemon_log.txt
✅ This completes the configuration, compilation, and execution documentation for all Lab 03 implementations.